Well, nethack may - deliberately - fail to guarantee a fair distribution 
of resources among different games, due to the simple fact that part of 
the creation of game resources is via a random number generator. 
However, I believe there should either be a way to create an - 
unattended - altar or there should be at least one guaranteed unattended 
altar in each game well above Orcus.  In my opinion, even if the game is 
not "fair" in a certain sense, it should provide the possibility for a 
resourceful player to advance in spite of bad rng luck.  Games happen 
where there is no altar besides the mine town one on the first 20 
levels.  If you have a character with weak stats and don't find any 
exceptional items, you will simply not be able to go down further beyond 
a certain level without meeting your demise with almost certainty.

So I think that either there should be a guaranteed altar (without a 
priest) on the first 10 to 15 levels or a way to make one, involving 
items only that you can get with reasonable probability.  If you want to 
avoid it unbalancing the game, make it difficult or tedious enough so 
only a desperate player will be willing to try; one could have a ritual 
(similar to invocation) involving several items such as a scroll of 
earth, a potion of confusion *and* one of hallucination that both have 
to be quaffed within a certain number of turns and that would in 
addition leave the PC stunned for a while after having managed to create 
an altar.  The alignment of the altar could be random and the player 
obtain an alignment penalty, if it is not his own alignment, so he might 
not be able to immediately convert it.  (It would still be useful for 
identifying the buc state of items without making too many returns to 
mine town.)

> Getting good at winning nethack at all ever is
> already a huge challenge. 

No.  It is a matter of patience and careful play.  I don't consider 
myself a good player, but I had ascended all classes in NH3.1.3 within 
two years after starting to play (four of them in the first try). 
However, I like the game, because you don't have to react fast or to be 
skillful with the keyboard.  You need to think about strategy, and all 
the information is available to be able to make an educated choice.  I 
am also a particularly slow player and accept many detours to get 
everything I think I need to ascend.  There is a point in many games 
beyond which you cannot lose except with exceedingly small probability 
-- if you prepare well enough (in earlier versions of the game, this 
probability was even smaller, certain things being guaranteed to work 
with 100% efficiency (unicorn horns, thrown potions of paralysis)).

> Having the game give you
> only paltry "stuff", and being able to win despite
> that,  is a higher level of challenge, thus Wade's

I think whoever wants particular challenges can have them by playing 
conducts.  There are terribly difficult ones available, and you can 
combine them (atheist, foodless, without magic resistance or whatever)...

> recent "but I can't catch you, this is already my
> third caveman", a way to tell apart players at the
> very top levels of play. Some of that ability to do
> fine discriminations should remain in the vanilla
> game, or all we have left is conducts.

I think the YANI would not take away these fine discriminations.  It 
would be a minor adjustment, making winning slightly less a matter of 
randomness and slightly more a matter of good play.  I don't think there 
is any need to make the game more difficult in the initial phase (one 
can always argue about the middle and end games, for players reaching 
them have already shown a certain level of perseverance, but whoever 
runs a game for 40000 turns should not depend on luck for winning 
anymore; anything that would reduce your winning probability to 50% at 
that stage, no matter how skilled you play would destroy the appeal of 
the game for me; a 1% inescapable failure chance beyond that point would 
be o.k. with me).  Instead, I would find changes interesting that make 
you less dependent on the rng in the early stage (and more dependent on 
your own ideas).  So changes that lead to additional ways, in the early 
game, of improving your chances to get to ascendable status would be 
welcome to me.  There should be sufficiently many ways to achieve a 
particular goal (in this case, sacrificing for an artifact or praying 
for holy water) that you can find one, even if the rng is not smiling at 
you.
